## Synthesis of an $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC MOF
${\mathrm{AgNO}}_{3}$ ( $\mathrm{160~mg)}$ and $\mathrm{AgS^{\mathrm{t}}B u}$ $(200~\mathrm{mg})$ were added to a mixed solution of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ $\mathrm{30~mL}$ and toluene $(30~\mathrm{mL})$ , respectively, fully dissolved and divided into 12 vials. After 4–5 days of volatilization, colorless bulk crystals appeared at the bottom. The crystals were filtered and added into a mixture of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ (2 mL) and toluene $(2~\mathrm{mL})$ with BMPTC $(8~\mathrm{mg})$ . After 3–4 days of evaporation, dark hexagonal crystals appeared at the bottom (yield: $78.5\%$ based on Ag). Elemental analysis: according to formula $\mathrm{Ag_{12}C_{186}O_{12}N_{12}S_{18}F_{18}H_{186}},$ the calculated value $(\%)$ : C 44.72; N 3.36; S 11.56; H 3.75. Experimental values $(\%)$ : C 44.53; N 3.45; S 11.92; H 3.59.
## Preparation of the photochromic patterns
A fine powder crystal of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC was printed on filter paper by covering a stencil and using a high-speed camera (1000 fps) to capture the color change during continuous UVlamp irradiation for $5s$ . Alternatively, the crystal was removed after $60~\mathsf{s}$ under UV-light irradiation, resulting in a distinct Chinese knot pattern printed in purple.
A compostie of fine powder crystal $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC and vacuum grease was created using a wet-chemical process method. A school text, badge, and trees were printed using the $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}.$ BMPTC/vacuum grease mixture and vacuum grease as confusion labels. These patterns were irradiated under a $365\mathrm{-nm~UV}$ lamp for $1\mathrm{min}$ , and the color transitioned from white to dark purple. After $30~\mathrm{min}$ irradiation with ${500}\mathrm{-nm}$ light, the darkpurple patterns returned to their original color.
## Characterization
SCXRD data were collected using a Bruker APEX-II CCD diffractometer with Mo-Kα radiation $(\lambda=0.71073\mathrm{~\AA~})$ at $200~\mathrm{K}$ . The structure was assessed with direct methods (SHELXS) and refined using full-matrix least squares in $F^{2}$ using the OLEX2, which utilizes the SHELXL-2015 module. The DIAMOND 3.2 software was used to visualize the crystal structure. The powder XRD (PXRD) patterns of the samples were obtained using a $\mathrm{D}/$ Max-3D diffractometer (Cu Kα, $\overset{\circ}{\lambda}\ =\ 1.5418\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were obtained and analyzed using an ALPHA II spectrometer. Photochromic ligands change before and after UV irradiation. Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed using a TANETZSCH STA 2500 thermal analyzer from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a heating rate of $\mathrm{i0^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ in a nitrogen atmosphere (flow rate of $60{\mathrm{~mL~min}}^{-2}$ ). A camera was used to capture the color transition. Solid-state UV-vis diffuse-reflectance spectra were recorded using a Hitachi UH4150 UV-vis spectrophotometer. The $365\mathrm{-nm}$ UV lamp $(8.3\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) and ${500-}\mathrm{nm}$ lamp $(3.5\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) were used.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Characterization of the photochromic linker and framework
## Photophysical properties of the photochromic $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC framework
The photoswitching characteristics of diarylethene molecules are easily identified because of their facile structural transformation during photoisomerization. BMPTC exhibits two photoisomeric forms: open (colorless) and closed (colored). Upon irradiation of the open form with UV light $(365\mathrm{nm})$ , a closed form between two heterocyclic thiophene groups was obtained. Notably, the BMPTC photoswitching is quite stable in both forms. This stability is mainly attributed to the $\"{}\mathrm{arm}\"{}$ of BMPTC, which exhibits relatively small changes in the same plane during isomerization and does not undergo drastic structural changes, especially in the solid state. Consequently, integrating into the SCC-MOF framework facilitates enhanced tunability and a multifaceted stimulus-responsive nature. As depicted in Fig. 2, we studied the photochromic behavior of an isolated single crystal compared with a single crystal in the $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC framework. Upon irradiation of the single crystal of the open form of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC with UV light, dark-purple crystals were readily observed without magnificaiton, suggesting the closure of the photochromic linker. After exposing these deep-purple crystals to visible light $(\lambda\ge500\mathrm{nm})$ , the linker reverted to its open state. This is confirmed by the recovery of the mauve color, observed after approximately $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The single-crystal structure of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}.$ - BMPTC was almost completely maintained upon alternating irradiation, thus demonstrating the excellent structural reversibility of the BMPTC linker within the framework. The PXRD and infrared spectroscopy revealed that the structural profiles of the open and closed forms remained almost unchanged, underscoring the maintained structural integrity of the framework (Figs S6 and S7). However, XRD analysis reveals that the dark-purple crystal structure displays a disordered form of the
BMPTC linker. This observation suggests that UV light may have limited penetration depth, thus only impacting the surfacelevel photochromic behavior of the crystal.
As with many diarylethene derivatives, the solid-state form of $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC MOF exhibits rapid photochromic properties at room temperature. Fig. 3a shows that the color shifts from milky white to deep purple within only $1.5\:\mathrm{s}$ under irradiation with a $365\mathrm{-nm}$ UV lamp, which is noteworthy compared with other photochromic MOFs (Table S2). Remarkably, an ultrafast and distinct photochromic response was observed within an astonishingly short timeframe of $0.1\ \mathsf{s}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , captured using a high-speed camera capable of recording 1000 frames per second. In addition, we measured the photochromic properties of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC
MOFs using solid diffuse-reflectance spectroscopy (Fig. 3c). Before irradiation, the main absorption peak appeared at approximately $310\ \mathrm{nm}$ . In contrast, after $365\mathrm{-nm}$ light irradiation, a new absorption peak appeared at approximately $588~\mathrm{nm}$ . With prolonged illumination time, the absorption intensity at $588~\mathrm{nm}$ gradually increased, while the intensity at $310\mathrm{nm}$ decreased, marked by the gradual deepening of the color of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ - BMPTC.
Interestingly, the photochromic dark-purple sample of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}\mathbf{-}$ BMPTC (closed form) can be reverted to an initial milky white state by visible light above $500\mathrm{nm}$ or grinding (Fig. 3b, c). Compared with the BMPTC linker, $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC has a faster photoresponse time (Figs S8–S10). Furthermore, we investigated the fatigue resistance of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC under alternating exposure to $365\mathrm{-nm}$ light for 1 min and ${500-}\mathrm{nm}$ light for $30\mathrm{min}$ . As illustrated in Fig. 3d, the photochromic properties of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}.$ - BMPTC remained favorable after 50 cycles upon alternating illumination, suggesting satisfactory photochromic stability. The PXRD patterns and FT-IR spectra of $_{\mathrm{Ag_{12}-B M P T C}}$ exhibited no significant difference before and after irradiation (Figs S6 and S7), indicating that the illumination decolorates the sample surface but does not change the internal framework of the crystal.
We further evaluated the practical application potential of $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC for information security, anticounterfeiting and encryption technologies. The evaluation was based on the reversible photochromic properties of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC under alternating irradiation of 365 and $500\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4a). First, we prepared $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC/vacuum grease composites using a wetchemical process method. Subsequently, we printed a potential information security and anticounterfeiting pattern using the $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC/vacuum grease mixture as the ink. A school text, badge, and trees were printed using the $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC/vacuum grease mixture. In addition, we selected vacuum grease as a confusion label. Fig. 4b–d show faint patterns on the printed paper, approaching milky white when viewed in daylight. Under irradiation with the $365\mathrm{-nm}$ UV lamp, dark-purple patterns appeared, clearly observable with the naked eye. In particular, the landscape painting with trees, wild goose, and deer patterns stood vividly on the printed paper. After $30\mathrm{min}$ irradiation with ${500-}\mathrm{nm}$ light, the dark-purple patterns returned to their original color. Furthermore, leveraging the advantage of photochromic functionality, we devised an encryption system based on $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}\mathbf{-}$ BMPTC. For the purpose, the $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC/vacuum grease mixture and vacuum grease were patterned into an 8888-shaped module. They demonstrated identical optical appearance in ambient light but presented the decrypted security codes $^{\mathfrak{a}}2023^{\mathfrak{v}}$ and $^{\alpha}1956^{\gamma}$ under $365\mathrm{-nm}$ light irradiation (Fig. 4e). Additionally, a sea landscape painting was presented by modulating $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}.$ - BMPTC photochromic behavior, containing incomplete, false, and correct information (Fig. 4f). In conclusion, the $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}{\mathrm{-BMPT}}$ material is a promising candidate for information security and encryption technologies and has the potential to be deployed as a reversible processable pigment.
Owing to its reversible photochromic effect, $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC was combined with a chiral scaffold, demonstrating potential chiral switch properties. When achiral $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC was introduced into the previously reported chiral medium of chiral binaphthalene di-octadecamidemethoxy ( $\cdot_{R^{-}}$ or S-BDA) [33] a chiral composite was obtained as a chiral film (Fig. S11). This chiral film exhibited either left-handed or right-handed chiral signals of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC, respectively. We speculate that the source of the chirality of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC is mainly associated with the supramolecular chirality induction facilitated by $R\cdot$ - or S-BDA. More importantly, the chiral composite has favorable chiral fatigue resistance, indicating its potential as a chiral switch. This study is the first example of SCC-MOFs introduced into chiral media to generate chirality switches.
## CONCLUSIONS
A novel 2D SCC-MOF framework ( $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC) was constructed using a ligand-exchange strategy at room temperature. $\mathrm{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC displays reversible photochromic behavior from milky white to dark purple upon exposure to daylight, UV-light irradiation, and grinding. Intriguingly, the photoresponse time is only 0.1 s under $365\mathrm{-nm}$ irradiation. The photochromic mechanism can be ascribed to the photoisomerization of the BMPTC ligand. Furthermore, $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}$ -BMPTC exhibits excellent reversible photoswitching properties at alternating irradiation of 365 and $500\mathrm{nm}$ . These attributes hold promise for applications in information securtity and anticounterfeiting and chirality switches. This study enriches the diversity and performance range of SCC-MOFs and provides a new strategy for designing and synthesizing novel photochromic materials.
